# Usage

This plugin creates a Changes Report from a `changes.xml` file, issue reports from JIRA, GitHub or Trac, and a
release announcement text. It can also send the announcement by email.

How to Generate the Changes Report
----------------------------------

Create a `changes.xml` file in the `src/changes/` directory. Here's an example of a typical `changes.xml`, showing
the syntax:

```xml
<document xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/changes/2.0.0"
          xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
          xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/changes/2.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/changes-2.0.0.xsd">
  <properties>
    <title>Changes Tester Project</title>
    <author email="jsmith@example.com">John Smith</author>
  </properties>
  <body>
    <release version="1.1" date="2026-03-01" description="Subsequent release">
      <action dev="jsmith" type="add">
        Added additional documentation on how to configure the plugin.
      </action>
      <action dev="jdoe" type="fix" issue="PROJ-11">
        Enable retrieving component-specific issues.
      </action>
      <action dev="jsmith" type="remove" due-to="Jane Roe, Joe Bloggs" due-to-email="jane@example.com, joe@example.com">
        Deleted the erroneous code.
      </action>
      <action dev="jsmith" type="update" fixed-issues="PROJ-12,PROJ-13">
        Updated the default announcement template.
      </action>
    </release>

    <release version="1.0" date="2026-01-01" description="First release">
      <action dev="jsmith" type="update">
        Uploaded documentation on how to use the plugin.
      </action>
    </release>
  </body>
</document>
```

See the [Changes Reference](./changes.html) for details regarding the `<release>` and `<action>` elements and their
attributes. The supported action types are `add`, `fix`, `update` and `remove`.

To generate the Changes Report, insert the Changes Plugin in the `<reporting>` section of your project's `pom.xml`

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-changes-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <reportSets>
          <reportSet>
            <reports>
              <report>changes</report>
            </reports>
          </reportSet>
        </reportSets>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting>
  ...
</project>
```

and execute the `site` phase to generate the report.

```
mvn site
```

You can also run the report on its own; it is written to `target/reports/changes.html`:

```
mvn changes:changes
```

#[[### Linking to Your Issue Management System]]#

If you use the `issue` or `fixed-issues` attribute in your `changes.xml` file and have the `<issueManagement>`
element configured in your `pom.xml`, the report links each issue to your issue management system.

The plugin comes pre-configured for many issue management systems. All you have to do is enter your issue management
system and its URL in your POM. It can look like this:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <issueManagement>
    <system>JIRA</system>
    <url>https://jira.example.com/browse/PROJ</url>
  </issueManagement>
  ...
</project>
```

The following table shows the pre-configured issue management systems and the templates they use to create links
from your Changes Report directly to the issues in your issue management system. The value of
`<issueManagement>/<system>` must match the name in the first column, ignoring case.

|System|Issue Link Template|
|:---|:---|
|Bitbucket|%URL%/issue/%ISSUE%|
|Bugzilla|%URL%/show_bug.cgi?id=%ISSUE%|
|GitHub|%URL%/%ISSUE%|
|GoogleCode|%URL%/detail?id=%ISSUE%|
|JIRA|%URL%/%ISSUE%|
|Mantis|%URL%/view.php?id=%ISSUE%|
|MKS|%URL%/viewissue?selection=%ISSUE%|
|Redmine|%URL%/issues/show/%ISSUE%|
|Scarab|%URL%/issues/id/%ISSUE%|
|SourceForge|`http://sourceforge.net/support/tracker.php?aid=%ISSUE%`|
|SourceForge2|%URL%/%ISSUE%|
|Trac|%URL%/ticket/%ISSUE%|
|Trackplus|%URL%/printItem.action?key=%ISSUE%|
|Tuleap|%URL%/?aid=%ISSUE%|
|YouTrack|%URL%/issue/%ISSUE%|

If you use an issue management system other than the ones above, or need to link to more than one, you need to
[configure an issue link template for it](./changes-mojo.html${esc.hash}issueLinkTemplatePerSystem). We would love
to extend the table above with more issue management systems, so if you have a working configuration that is not
listed above, please tell us about it by [creating an issue for it](./issue-management.html).

**Note:** `%URL%` is the value of `<issueManagement>/<url>` with the last `/` and everything after it removed. For
JIRA, `https://jira.example.com/browse/PROJ` gives links like `https://jira.example.com/browse/PROJ-11`. For GitHub,
end the URL with a slash, as in `https://github.com/example/project/issues/`, so that the links point to
`https://github.com/example/project/issues/11`; without the trailing slash the `issues` part is dropped.

How to Generate the JIRA Report
-------------------------------

**Note:** To use the JIRA Report, the `<issueManagement>` section in the `pom.xml` of your project must be
configured. It might look something like this:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <issueManagement>
    <system>JIRA</system>
    <url>https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/MCHANGES</url>
  </issueManagement>
  ...
</project>
```

The plugin reads the issues through the JIRA REST API. To generate the JIRA Report, insert the Changes Plugin in the
`<reporting>` section of your project's `pom.xml`

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-changes-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <reportSets>
          <reportSet>
            <reports>
              <report>jira-changes</report>
            </reports>
          </reportSet>
        </reportSets>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting>
  ...
</project>
```

and execute the site goal to generate the report.

```
mvn site
```

For info on how to modify the JIRA Report see the [Customizing the JIRA Report](./examples/customizing-jira-report.html)
example.

How to Generate the GitHub Report
---------------------------------

**Note:** To use the GitHub Report, the `<issueManagement>` section in the `pom.xml` of your project must be
configured. It might look something like this:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <issueManagement>
    <system>GitHub</system>
    <url>https://github.com/apache/maven-changes-plugin/issues/</url>
  </issueManagement>
  ...
</project>
```

To generate the GitHub Report, insert the Changes Plugin in the `<reporting>` section of your project's `pom.xml`

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-changes-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <reportSets>
          <reportSet>
            <reports>
              <report>github-changes</report>
            </reports>
          </reportSet>
        </reportSets>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting>
  ...
</project>
```

and execute the site goal to generate the report.

```
mvn site
```

For info on how to modify the GitHub Report see the [Configuring the GitHub Report](./examples/configuring-github-report.html)
example.

How to generate and send the Announcement via Email
---------------------------------------------------

The `announcement-generate` goal writes the announcement text, by default to
`target/announcement/announcement.vm`. Set `<announcementFile>` to choose another file name. By default the
announcement is built from the `changes.xml` file:

```
mvn changes:announcement-generate
```

To build the announcement from an issue tracker instead, list it in `<issueManagementSystems>`. Valid values are
`changes.xml`, `JIRA`, `GitHub` and `Trac`; the tracker must also be the one configured in `<issueManagement>`.
To generate the announcement based on **both** a `changes.xml` file and JIRA you should configure your `pom.xml`
like this. `<onlyCurrentVersion>` makes JIRA return the issues of the version you release; without it, the plugin
fetches only the latest `<maxEntries>` (25) issues, which might not include that version:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-changes-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <issueManagementSystems>
            <issueManagementSystem>changes.xml</issueManagementSystem>
            <issueManagementSystem>JIRA</issueManagementSystem>
          </issueManagementSystems>
          <onlyCurrentVersion>true</onlyCurrentVersion>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  ...
</project>
```

The announcement groups issues from a tracker by action type: _add_, _fix_, _update_ or _remove_. By default, for
JIRA, _add_ corresponds to 'New Feature', _fix_ to 'Bug', and _update_ to 'Improvement', 'Task', 'Wish' and
'Dependency upgrade'; for GitHub, _add_ corresponds to the label 'enhancement' and _fix_ to 'bug'. If your tracker uses
other issue types, map them with `<issueTypes>`, separating several types with commas:

```xml
<configuration>
  <issueTypes>
    <add>Story</add>
    <fix>Defect,Malfunction</fix>
  </issueTypes>
</configuration>
```

To send the announcement by email, configure the mail server and the recipients:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-changes-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <smtpHost>mail.example.com</smtpHost>
          <smtpPort>25</smtpPort>
          <toAddresses>
            <toAddress>users@example.com</toAddress>
            <toAddress>announce@example.com</toAddress>
          </toAddresses>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  ...
</project>
```

The `announcement-mail` goal runs `announcement-generate` first and then sends the result:

```
mvn changes:announcement-mail
```

For info on how to change the sender of the email see the [Specifying the Mail Sender](./examples/specifying-mail-sender.html)
example, and for a server that needs a login see [SMTP Authentication](./examples/smtp-authentication.html).
